@node LibreClaw Channels
@section Channels

LibreClaw exposes five channel kinds, all implemented as GObjects
behind the @code{LcChannel} interface.  cmacs-libreclaw treats them
uniformly — @code{cmacs-libreclaw-send-message} works against any of
them.

@subsection Cmacs (LcCmacsChannel) — the recommended default

The native in-process channel for embedded use inside Emacs.  The
Emacs host pushes inbound messages via
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-cmacs-channel-inject} and receives AI
responses through a registered callback that dispatches into the
right org-mode buffer.

Key properties:

@itemize
@item
Multi-room: a single channel instance tracks many rooms.  Each
Emacs project directory becomes its own room with its own AI
session context.
@item
Persistent: buffer history is saved per project under
@file{~/.libreclaw/cmacs-sessions/<sha>.org}, and libreclaw's own
session manager persists the AI context under the key
@code{cmacs:<project-root>:<user>}.  Both survive Emacs restarts.
@item
No credentials, no ports, no hostnames.  Just
@code{channels: cmacs: enabled: true} and it works.
@end itemize

@example
channels:
  cmacs:
    enabled: true
@end example

Usage from Emacs:

@example
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-open              ; opens the chat buffer for
                                      ; the current project root
@end example

@subsection Matrix (LcMatrixChannel)

Talks Matrix Client-Server API via libsoup3.  Supports long-poll
@code{/sync}, reply threading, room membership events.

@example
channels:
  - id: matrix
    kind: matrix
    homeserver:   "https://matrix.example.com"
    user_id:      "@@bot:example.com"
    access_token: "$@{auth-source:matrix:@@bot:example.com@}"
    auto_join:
      - "!general:example.com"
      - "!ops:example.com"
@end example

Runtime room-joining is not currently supported through cmacs — use
@code{auto_join} and reload.

@subsection Local (LcLocalChannel)

stdin/stdout channel — reads from fd 0, writes to fd 1.  Useful
for the standalone @command{libreclaw} CLI but @emph{not} for
embedded use inside Emacs (Emacs's stdin/stdout is not a chat
transport).  Use the Cmacs channel above instead.

@example
channels:
  local:
    enabled: true
    prompt: "libreclaw> "
@end example

@subsection Email (LcEmailChannel)

IMAP/SMTP via libetpan.

@example
channels:
  - id: email
    kind: email
    imap_host: "imap.example.com"
    smtp_host: "smtp.example.com"
    username:  "bot@@example.com"
    password:  "$@{auth-source:email:bot@@example.com@}"
@end example

@subsection Webhook (LcWebhookChannel)

HTTP webhook receiver.

@example
channels:
  - id: webhook
    kind: webhook
    port: 8080
    path_prefix: "/libreclaw/webhooks"
@end example

@subsection Channel DEFUNs (cross-channel)

@table @code
@item cmacs-libreclaw-list-channels
Return @code{((id . kind) @dots{})} of active channels.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-channel-kind
Kind symbol for a given channel id (@samp{matrix}, @samp{local},
@samp{cmacs}, @samp{email}, @samp{webhook}).
@item cmacs-libreclaw-channel-connected-p
Connection state.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-channel-connect
Async connect.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-channel-disconnect
Async disconnect.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-send-message
Send a message on any channel kind.
@end table

@subsection Cmacs channel DEFUNs (native-only)

@table @code
@item cmacs-libreclaw-cmacs-channel-available-p
@code{t} if the channel is bound.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-cmacs-channel-inject
Inject an inbound message (room, sender, body).
@item cmacs-libreclaw-cmacs-channel-register-room
Add a room to the known-rooms set.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-cmacs-channel-unregister-room
Drop a room from the known-rooms set.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-cmacs-channel-list-rooms
List known rooms.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-cmacs-channel-has-room-p
Check room membership.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-open
Elisp entry point --- open the chat buffer for the current
project root.
@end table
